# React 19 usage

The React Compiler is enabled (`babel-plugin-react-compiler`). Default to
plain functions and inline literals. Do NOT hand-write `useMemo` /
`useCallback` / `React.memo` for performance — the compiler does it. Keep
a manual memo only for a concrete identity contract (effect dep, ref
callback, imperatively-attached listener, third-party reference compare)
and name the consumer in a one-line comment. See `typescript-style.mdc`.

Reach for these primitives only when the specific need below applies:

- **`useSyncExternalStore`** — subscribe to a store that lives outside
  React (the layout machine, a Tauri `listen(...)` event stream). The
  correct home for high-frequency external truth; avoids tearing.
- **`useEffectEvent`** (stable in 19.2) — call the latest props/state from
  *inside* an Effect without listing them as deps, so the Effect doesn't
  re-fire when they change. This is the "no-stale-deps" event hook. Only
  callable inside an Effect — not a DOM handler like `onClick`.
- **`useTransition` / `startTransition`** — mark a non-urgent UI swap
  (opening a panel, switching a view) so input stays responsive while the
  next render prepares.
- **`useDeferredValue`** — defer a slow subtree (filtered/virtualized
  list) behind a fast input value.
- **`useOptimistic`** — instant UI feedback while a Tauri/server mutation
  is in flight.
- **`useActionState`** — pending + result + error for an async action
  (settings forms, etc.).
- **`use()`** — unwrap a promise/context conditionally in render.
